<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Micipsa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 118 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Micipsa was king of Numidia from 148 to 118 BCE, the eldest legitimate son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a>. He first shared power with his brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulussa">Gulussa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mastanabal">Mastanabal</a> and, after their deaths, ruled alone from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a>, keeping the kingdom a loyal ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and encouraging Greek and Punic culture. He adopted his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha">Jugurtha</a> and left the kingdom jointly to him and his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adherbal">Adherbal</a> and Hiempsal, a settlement that soon collapsed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/micipsa.jpg" alt="Micipsa" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bibliothèque nationale de France, département Monnaies, médailles et antiques, Luynes 3958 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tridrachme,_Argent,_Numidie,_Micipsa2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
